Some materials about successful industrial/applied research labs. I recommend all of them. Further recommendations very welcome. Also, does it just seem that their heyday is past, or has something structurally changed?
 linkify false
 link mailto:patrick@collison.ie welcome
 italics seem

- Dealers of Lightning. The definitive book about PARC.
 linkify false
 link https://www.amazon.com/Dealers-Lightning-Xerox-PARC-Computer/dp/0887309895 Dealers of Lightning

- Inside PARC: the 'information' architects (IEEE Spectrum, Oct 1985). Good article about PARC.
 linkify false
 link https://spectrum.ieee.org/ns/pdfs/inside-the-parc.pdf Inside PARC: the 'information' architects

- Interview with Bob Taylor (and another), who ran the PARC CS Lab.
 linkify false
 link http://archive.computerhistory.org/resources/text/Oral_History/Taylor_Robert/102702015.05.01.acc.pdf Interview with Bob Taylor
 link https://patrickcollison.com/static/files/labs/taylor-markoff-interview.pdf another

- The Idea Factory. The definitive book about Bell Labs. (There should be more...)
 linkify false
 link https://www.amazon.com/Idea-Factory-Great-American-Innovation/dp/0143122797 The Idea Factory

- The Art of Doing Science and Engineering. Only indirectly about Bell Labs but so good that you should read it anyway.
 linkify false
 link https://www.amazon.com/Art-Doing-Science-Engineering-Learning/dp/9056995006 The Art of Doing Science and Engineering

- Tuxedo Park. Book about the MIT Rad Lab, among other things. (Also worth reading Endless Frontier. Broader influence of NDRC is underestimated, as far as I can tell.)
 linkify false
 link https://www.amazon.com/Tuxedo-Park-Street-Science-Changed/dp/0684872889 Tuxedo Park
 link https://www.amazon.com/Endless-Frontier-Vannevar-Engineer-American/dp/0262740222 Endless Frontier
 link https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/National_Defense_Research_Committee NDRC

- MIT's Building 20: "The Magical Incubator". Transcript of a talk about Building 20.
 linkify false
 link https://infinitehistory.mit.edu/video/mits-building-20-magical-incubator MIT's Building 20: "The Magical Incubator"
 link https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Building_20 Building 20

- Funding Breakthrough Research: Promises and Challenges of the “ARPA Model”. An analysis of what the ARPA model is and why it might work.
 linkify false
 link http://mitsloan.mit.edu/shared/ods/documents/?DocumentID=4615 Funding Breakthrough Research: Promises and Challenges of the “ARPA Model”

- The Dream Machine. Book about ARPA, Licklider, and the creation of the internet.
 linkify false
 link https://www.amazon.com/Dream-Machine-Licklider-Revolution-Computing/dp/014200135X The Dream Machine

- The Power of the Context. Alan Kay's reflections on ARPA and PARC.
 linkify false
 link https://patrickcollison.com/static/files/labs/context.pdf The Power of the Context

- The Making of the Atomic Bomb. Book about the Manhattan Project.
 linkify false
 link https://www.amazon.com/Making-Atomic-Bomb-25th-Anniversary/dp/1451677618/ The Making of the Atomic Bomb

- Skunk Works. The Lockheed Martin facility behind the U-2, SR-71, etc. (See also: Kelly Johnson's 14 Rules, Kelly Johnson's own memoir, Augustine's Laws, Boyd, and National Defense.)
